#!/bin/sh
# WRITE-TIME REUSE GATE (AO2, 2026-08-08, DIRECTOR_PROGRAMME_ARCHITECTED_OUT_2026-08-05 step MAP):
# a commit that ADDS a new capability module must carry a REUSE record saying what the capability
# index answered and, under the three ruled part classes, what the ecosystem answered. The director
# names this "the only immediate behaviour change" in the programme; AO1's index is a demo until
# this spends it.
#
# WHY commit-msg AND NOT pre-commit: the message does not exist when pre-commit runs (git writes it
# at prepare-commit-msg, afterwards), so a pre-commit reading the record would read the PREVIOUS
# commit's message and pass -- fail-open, and silently.
#
# It refuses a missing RECORD, never a decision to write new code: forced reuse that couples two
# purposes is the mirror error of duplication and is equally a defect (director's wall). A commit
# adding no new .py under a code root is never touched, in any state. De-fang a false positive by
# writing 'warn' to tools/write_time_gate.mode -- a deliberate one-line diff, never automatic.
# R15-proven both ways in tests/tools/test_write_time_gate.py (8 source mutations).
python3 tools/write_time_gate.py "$1" || exit 1

# NEXT-STEP GATE (director console, 2026-09-05): a commit that advances an OPEN atom must record
# what comes next, as an atom id or an explicit `none -- <reason>`. Its sibling above refuses a
# commit that ADDS a capability without saying what it reused; this one refuses a commit that
# ADVANCES one without saying what follows. The failure it exists to stop was measured: a next
# step named in a session report existed in a chat window and nowhere in the queue, so nothing
# could draw it, and the queue went on offering machinery because machinery was what was in it.
python3 tools/next_step_gate.py "$1" || exit 1
